WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.979 --> 00:00:18.003
November 15 Dear Daddy Longlegs, I've changed my name!

2
00:00:19.644 --> 00:00:21.365
I'm still Jerusha to the college.

3
00:00:22.926 --> 00:00:24.988
But I'm Judy to everyone else.

4
00:00:26.309 --> 00:00:28.370
Usually somebody else comes up with a nickname.

5
00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.260
(Just like I gave you the name Daddy Longlegs!)

6
00:00:33.521 --> 00:00:36.483
So I guess it's sad that I had to make one up for myself.

7
00:00:37.724 --> 00:00:39.065
But that's not totally true.

8
00:00:41.046 --> 00:00:43.448
One of the little orphans used to call me Judy.

9
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.431
He couldn't say Jerusha when he was learning to talk.

10
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:52.234
I always wondered where my name came from.

11
00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:56.397
One day, when I still lived at the orphanage, I asked Mrs.

12
00:00:56.437 --> 00:01:00.140
Lippett. "Did my parents name me Jerusha Abbott?"

13
00:01:01.984 --> 00:01:03.005
Mrs. Lippett frowned.

14
00:01:03.025 --> 00:01:04.465
"Oh no.

15
00:01:04.985 --> 00:01:06.906
You were like many of the other orphans here.

16
00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:10.188
We didn't know your parents' names or your name.

17
00:01:11.608 --> 00:01:14.109
Someone just left you on the orphanage steps."

18
00:01:17.951 --> 00:01:20.132
"Then where did my name come from?"

19
00:01:20.152 --> 00:01:24.974
"The same place I get all the orphans' names—the telephone book!"

20
00:01:26.415 --> 00:01:28.376
Mrs. Lippett sounded proud of herself.

21
00:01:29.592 --> 00:01:32.254
"You'll find Abbott right there on the first page."

22
00:01:34.837 --> 00:01:36.738
"But where did you find Jerusha?"

23
00:01:36.758 --> 00:01:40.061
"On a tombstone in the cemetery!

24
00:01:43.304 --> 00:01:46.126
Now get back to work, Jerusha, and stop asking questions."

25
00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:51.270
Well, Daddy Longlegs, I never told her this, but I'll tell you.

26
00:01:52.852 --> 00:01:55.214
I've always hated the name Jerusha.

27
00:01:56.034 --> 00:01:57.476
But I like Judy.

28
00:01:58.676 --> 00:01:59.757
It's such a silly name.

29
00:02:01.218 --> 00:02:04.001
It belongs to someone who is the complete opposite of me.

30
00:02:05.902 --> 00:02:10.226
A sweet little blue-eyed thing, whose family adores and spoils her.

31
00:02:11.728 --> 00:02:15.050
I may have faults, but no one can say that I've been spoiled.

32
00:02:16.352 --> 00:02:19.094
So from now on, please call me Judy.

33
00:02:20.215 --> 00:02:21.576
I have some more news for you.

34
00:02:23.638 --> 00:02:25.059
I made the basketball team!

35
00:02:26.664 --> 00:02:29.205
You should see the bruise I got the other day at practice.

36
00:02:30.245 --> 00:02:33.586
My left shoulder is blue and purple with orange streaks.

37
00:02:35.207 --> 00:02:38.848
For your information, Julia Pendleton didn't make the team.

38
00:02:40.128 --> 00:02:43.109
Hooray! (I really am very mean sometimes.)

39
00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:47.330
I like college more and more every day.

40
00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:53.932
I like the girls (except for Julia) and the teachers and the classes and the food.

41
00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.401
We have ice cream twice a week.

42
00:02:59.381 --> 00:03:03.482
We never have cornmeal mush, which we had often at the orphanage.

43
00:03:04.962 --> 00:03:06.623
Oh, I just remembered something!

44
00:03:07.843 --> 00:03:09.524
I never told you about my clothes.

45
00:03:10.624 --> 00:03:13.825
Before school started, Miss Pritchard took me shopping.

46
00:03:13.845 --> 00:03:19.266
(She's on the ladies' committee at the orphanage, but I guess you remember that.)

47
00:03:20.687 --> 00:03:23.207
Anyway, I thought I'd get one new dress.

48
00:03:24.288 --> 00:03:27.189
Well, maybe two since you were paying for them.

49
00:03:28.429 --> 00:03:34.351
But here's what happened: "I like this dress for classes," I said as we shopped.

50
00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:36.751
"And I like this one for church.

51
00:03:37.351 --> 00:03:38.132
May I get both?"

52
00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.413
Miss Pritchard smiled kindly at me.

53
00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:44.974
"You'll need more than two dresses at college."

54
00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:46.494
"I will?"

55
00:03:46.514 --> 00:03:48.354
"Yes, dear.

56
00:03:48.975 --> 00:03:53.496
You'll need a suit and an evening dress and at least one dinner dress."

57
00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.202
Who knew college required so many clothes?

58
00:03:59.324 --> 00:04:04.868
So, thanks to you, I now own a dress for classes, a dress for church, and a suit.

59
00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.493
Plus, two dinner dresses and a silk evening dress!

60
00:04:11.894 --> 00:04:15.157
Can you imagine me—an orphan—in an evening dress?

61
00:04:16.618 --> 00:04:19.781
I also have three pairs of fine white gloves.

62
00:04:20.942 --> 00:04:23.464
At the orphanage I received mittens for Christmas.

63
00:04:25.004 --> 00:04:27.745
But I never had beautiful gloves like these.

64
00:04:28.866 --> 00:04:31.147
I like them so much that I often try them on.

65
00:04:32.147 --> 00:04:33.568
I wish I could wear them to class!

66
00:04:35.089 --> 00:04:37.650
You probably think I'm behaving like a silly girl.

67
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.151
And that you're wasting your money educating me.

68
00:04:41.832 --> 00:04:47.074
But, Daddy Longlegs, if you'd worn blue gingham most of your life, you'd understand!

69
00:04:48.675 --> 00:04:51.096
When I started high school, things got even worse.

70
00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:54.722
My clothes came from the village poor box.

71
00:04:57.204 --> 00:05:01.768
On the first day of school, I sat next to a girl who used to own my dress.

72
00:05:01.788 --> 00:05:04.630
"Did you see her dress?"

73
00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:06.932
the girl next to me whispered to a friend.

74
00:05:06.952 --> 00:05:08.973
"Whose dress?"

75
00:05:08.993 --> 00:05:11.435
"The girl from the orphanage.

76
00:05:11.956 --> 00:05:15.839
Jerusha." The second girl studied me.

77
00:05:15.859 --> 00:05:20.122
"Didn't you have a dress like that last year?"

78
00:05:20.663 --> 00:05:21.804
she said to the first girl.

79
00:05:23.312 --> 00:05:29.915
"Yes! But it's out of style, so my mother put it in the poor box."

80
00:05:30.775 --> 00:05:33.236
Then they laughed while I turned red with shame.

81
00:05:34.457 --> 00:05:36.257
And this happened every year!

82
00:05:36.277 --> 00:05:39.779
I'll never forget that feeling.

83
00:05:41.299 --> 00:05:43.700
Do you see why I appreciate my new clothes?

84
00:05:46.001 --> 00:05:50.923
Six outfits—all pretty and new and bought just for me.

85
00:05:52.224 --> 00:05:55.794
One last thing: I know I shouldn't expect any letters from you.

86
00:05:56.895 --> 00:05:57.296
And Mrs.

87
00:05:57.336 --> 00:05:59.618
Lippett told me not to bother you with questions.

88
00:06:01.019 --> 00:06:03.061
But I just want to know a few things.

89
00:06:04.202 --> 00:06:06.725
Are you very old or just a little old?

90
00:06:07.686 --> 00:06:10.829
Are you completely bald or only a little bald?

91
00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:14.672
It's hard to picture you without some details.

92
00:06:15.613 --> 00:06:18.055
Your well-dressed student, Judy Abbott
